'''blarney''' (blär′ni), ''n.'' [Popularly referred to Castle ''Blarney'', near Cork in Ireland, in the wall of which is a stone (the “Blarney stone”) said to endow those who kiss it with unusual facility and unscrupulousness in the use of flattery and compliment.] Exceedingly complimentary language; flattery; smooth, wheedling talk; pleasing cajolery.
{{smaller block|The ''blarney'' ’s so great a deceiver. {{right|''S. Lover.''}}
Madame de Staël was regretting to Lord Castlereagh that there was no word in the English language which answered to their “Sentiment.” “No,” he said, “there is no English word, but the Irish have one that corresponds exactly,—''blarney!''” {{right|''Caroline Fox'', Journal, p. 121.}}}}

